      "text": "To solve for the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), we need to find the largest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\) satisfies the congruence relation:\n\n\\[\nn \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\n\\]\n\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be expressed in the form:\n\n\\[\nn = 22k + 15\n\\]\n\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We need to find the largest integer \\( n \\) that is a 4-digit number. Therefore, \\( n \\) must satisfy:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\leq n < 10000\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( n = 22k + 15 \\) into this inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15 < 10000\n\\]\n\nFirst, solve the lower bound:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15\n\\]\n\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n985 \\leq 22k\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 22:\n\n\\[\nk \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\approx 44.809\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.809, which is 45. Therefore:\n\n\\[\nk \\geq 45\n\\]\n\nNext, solve the upper bound:\n\n\\[\n22k + 15 < 10000\n\\]\n\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n22k < 9985\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 22:\n\n\\[\nk < \\frac{9985}{22} \\approx 453.409\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the largest integer less than 453.409, which is 453. Therefore:\n\n\\[\nk \\leq 453\n\\]\n\nThe largest integer \\( k \\) is 453. Substituting \\( k = 453 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nn = 22k + 15 = 22 \\times 453 + 15\n\\]\n\nCalculate the value:\n\n\\[\n22 \\times 453 = 9966\n\\]\n\n\\[\n22 \\times 453 + 15 = 9966 + 15 = 9981\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9981}\n\\]",
