      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the Problem:**\n   We are looking for a number \\( N \\) such that:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\n   \\]\n   This means that when \\( N \\) is divided by 22, the remainder is 15.\n\n2. **Express \\( N \\) in Terms of the Congruence:**\n   Let \\( N \\) be an integer. Then:\n   \\[\n   N = 22k + 15\n   \\]\n   for some integer \\( k \\).\n\n3. **Determine the Range of \\( N \\):**\n   Since \\( N \\) is a 4-digit integer, we have:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\leq N \\leq 9999\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( N = 22k + 15 \\) into the inequality, we get:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve the Inequality:**\n   First, solve the lower bound:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\leq 22k + 15\n   \\]\n   Subtract 15 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   985 \\leq 22k\n   \\]\n   Divide by 22:\n   \\[\n   k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\approx 44.7727\n   \\]\n   Since \\( k \\) must be an integer, we have:\n   \\[\n   k \\geq 45\n   \\]\n\n   Next, solve the upper bound:\n   \\[\n   22k + 15 \\leq 9999\n   \\]\n   Subtract 15 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   22k \\leq 9984\n   \\]\n   Divide by 22:\n   \\[\n   k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\approx 453.8182\n   \\]\n   Since \\( k \\) must be an integer, we have:\n   \\[\n   k \\leq 453\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the Largest Integer \\( k \\):**\n   The largest integer \\( k \\) that satisfies both \\( k \\geq 45 \\) and \\( k \\leq 453 \\) is \\( k = 453 \\).\n\n6. **Calculate \\( N \\):**\n   Substitute \\( k = 453 \\) back into the equation \\( N = 22k + 15 \\):\n   \\[\n   N = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\( 22 \\cdot 453 \\):\n   \\[\n   22 \\cdot 453 = 10106\n   \\]\n   Add 15:\n   \\[\n   N = 10106 + 15 = 10121\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest 4-digit integer congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is \\(\\boxed{10121}\\).",
